00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.290
of the Center for Quantum
Network in Arizona

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.880
and the Yale Quantum
Institute in Connecticut.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:35.520
So this is the second panel.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.900
And today we are focusing on
How to Prepare for Grad School.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.790
We wanted to offer this panel

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.690
since building quantum technology
requires a lot of people

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:44.600
with different backgrounds,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.460
we need physicists, we
need electrical engineer,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.760
we need computer scientists,
software engineer, chemists

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.360
and it can be really overwhelming

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.963
to consider a career
in quantum information.

00:00:54.978 --> 00:00:57.240
So today we will hear from grad students

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.180
and grad school administrator and Dean

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.500
and what it's like to work in quantum

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.560
and get advice on how to
navigate the various pathway

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.530
to do a career in the
field of quantum science

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.363
and information.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.880
I would like to introduce
my colleague Russell Balliet

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.080
who is the Assistant Director

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.780
of the Center for Quantum Network

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.290
and will serve as a co-moderator.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:19.520
And so I will let him
introduce himself briefly

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.190
before we welcome our panelists.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:22.970
- Yep, so thanks Florian.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:26.140
So like said, I'm the Assistant Director

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.870
for Engineering Workforce Development

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.680
for the Center of Quantum Networks

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.610
so a lot of you are
already familiar with me

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:37.080
and I'm also managing a couple
of summer programs this year,

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.850
RUs and RUT.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.570
So I'll be moderating as well.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:45.570
And might chime in with my
graduate school stories.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.690
Thanks Florian.

