00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.610
So let's start by let's do Emily

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:47.480
do you want to introduce yourself

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.179
so you can talk a little
bit about yourself

00:02:49.179 --> 00:02:52.370
and why you wanted to go
into the quantum fields

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.370
and what it's like to be in grad school.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.660
- Okay hi, I'm Emily Van Milligen.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.700
I'm a fourth year graduate student

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.870
at the University of Arizona

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:06.330
where I'm currently
getting my PhD in physics.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:11.330
I work in Saikat Guha's Quantum
Photonics Applications Group

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.950
he's actually in the Optics Department

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.560
which is nice because at the UoA

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.080
you're encouraged to work with professors

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.840
in different departments
than where you are in.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.870
And I primarily focus

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:29.793
on studying the behavior
of quantum networks.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:35.800
So I've been interested
in quantum mechanics

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.910
since I went to undergrad
at Tufts University.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.560
And so I really enjoyed
my undergrad classes

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.140
and I just knew I wanted to take more.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.030
I didn't really know if I loved research

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.020
or what I wanted to do,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.320
I just knew that I wanted to
continue on to grad school

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:54.010
because I wanted to learn
more about the fields

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.620
and see what opportunities were out there.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.270
And it was a really nice fit

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:01.740
going to the University of Arizona,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.650
because at least in the physics department

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:09.650
they give you a couple
years to jump around groups

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:11.400
and find your actual fit.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.400
So it's very flexible and it's nice

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.200
because as I was working
towards completing my masters,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:19.530
taking my classes

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:22.510
I was getting to sample
all these different groups

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:26.930
and I found my home with Professor Saikat

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:30.920
and I really enjoy working with him.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:35.160
I think it's fun because
the whole quantum world

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.760
like plays by a different set of rules

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.500
than like the world we interact
with on a day-to-day basis

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.840
and so that kind of just turns everything

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:45.650
into a giant puzzle for me.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:50.057
So it's not really so much
work as it is like fun.

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:54.336
- Thank you.

00:04:54.336 --> 00:04:56.410
Connor, do you want to go next?

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:57.243
- Sure.

00:04:57.243 --> 00:04:58.076
Hi everyone.

00:04:58.076 --> 00:04:58.909
My name is Connor Hann.

00:04:58.909 --> 00:05:01.880
I am a, well, no longer a graduate student

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:02.760
at Yale University

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.830
I actually just defended my
thesis a couple of weeks ago.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:08.203
So I just finished up
after five years at Yale.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:13.620
I was advised there by
Professor Steve Girvin

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.990
and also co-advised by
Professor Liang Jang

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:18.870
at the University of Chicago.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.070
So my journey into quantum

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:23.080
was perhaps a little bit more meandering

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.430
I definitely didn't know starting out,

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:26.820
even that I wanted to do physics

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:28.500
let alone quantum information.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.330
When I started my undergraduate
at Duke University

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.560
I actually thought I wanted
to go be a medical doctor

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.450
but lo and behold there
were no spots available

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:38.717
in the first year bio class

00:05:38.717 --> 00:05:40.330
and so I took physics instead

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:43.390
and then from there I realized
that that was the subject

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:44.860
I think I liked a lot more.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.360
So I kept taking physics
courses and so on and so forth

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.770
until I found myself in quantum
mechanics in my junior year.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:52.450
And once I'd taken quantum

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:54.620
it was really kind of
a no going back moment.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.480
I sorta tried to shift
my research interests

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.860
in that direction and learn
more and more about the field.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:03.580
But even by the time it came
to apply to graduate school

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:04.750
I had done some research

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:07.220
broadly in the field of quantum physics

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:09.360
but nothing with respect
to say quantum computing

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.930
or quantum information.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.550
And so it really took
me even into grad school

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:14.700
to learn a whole lot

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.900
about the the field of quantum computing

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.774
where I now have done most of my research.

00:06:19.774 --> 00:06:21.850
And I spent, you know, maybe a year or so

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:24.210
thinking about trying out
different groups at Yale

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.070
and figuring out what
would be a nice fit for me

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:28.430
but I was really happy ultimately

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:31.180
to wind up in the groups of
Steve Girvin and Liang Jang

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:34.690
because for me, one thing that
I really value in my research

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.270
is feeling a close connection

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.860
to practical applications
in the real world.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:41.330
And so this is something I
felt with quantum computing.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:44.120
You know, I could see the
tangible potential impacts

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.260
of the work I was doing on the day-to-day.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:47.713
It felt more, you know

00:06:47.713 --> 00:06:50.990
in touch with developing
technology and things like that.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:53.250
So for me, that's always
been a nice aspect

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:54.140
of working in the field

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:55.890
and something that I've really enjoyed

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.860
and more broadly, yeah,
I've had a really fun time

00:06:58.860 --> 00:06:59.693
studying quantum

00:06:59.693 --> 00:07:02.560
it's been a really exciting
subject to pursue my PhD

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.850
and I'll be excited to continue
researching in the field

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:06.683
afterwards.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:09.163
- Thanks a lot.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:11.727
Ann, do you want to go next?

00:07:11.727 --> 00:07:14.090
- Sure, my name is Ann Gaylin.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:16.570
I'm an Associate Dean at Yale

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:20.890
and my portfolio contains a lot of things,

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:25.500
but I work with our graduate
programs on policies,

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.990
curriculum and annual reviews

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:32.270
for about 40 of our PhD programs.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:33.550
I also work directly

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:37.080
with the faculty heads
of the graduate programs

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:40.390
what we call the Directors
of Graduate Study.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:43.510
And I also work with some
of our graduate programs

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:44.920
on the admissions process.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:49.750
So, first of all, I'm not
here to talk about quantum,

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:51.160
I know nothing about quantum,

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:54.710
so I will defer to the others
on the panel about that.

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:57.620
But I'm really here to
answer any general questions

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:00.030
you might have about graduate school.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:01.770
And some of the things
you might want to ask me

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:04.160
about, what should I be thinking about

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:08.740
or preparing in order to
apply if I've decided to go?

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:11.730
Why might I want to go to graduate school?

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.380
What makes for a good application?

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:16.913
What should I know once I get here?

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:20.997
You know, how do you navigate
what some people call

00:08:20.997 --> 00:08:24.040
"the hidden curriculum
of graduate school"?

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.380
And what kinds of
assistance either financial

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:29.390
or otherwise are available
in graduate school

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:31.250
to help me achieve my goals

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:34.240
and succeed in whatever
I've come to graduate school

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:35.630
to learn.

00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:36.740
So I'll stop there

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:38.993
and look forward to
hearing your questions.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.380
- Thank you.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:45.670
So we can start by choosing the poll

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:48.160
and I can also share the results.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.860
And we see that
everybody's very interested

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:54.920
in having like in STEM
careers, which is great for us.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.490
However, we have like a
moderate understanding

00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:59.560
on like the information on
the career so that's perfect

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:02.090
because we're gonna be able
to to help you figure out

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:05.300
if going to grad school
is the right move for you.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:08.352
There's a turn off of way to
join the Quantum Workforce

00:09:08.352 --> 00:09:10.940
and you can also see that all the panels

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.490
that we have in this series

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.040
we go from not only to grad school

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.110
to do a professorship

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.780
but also joining an
industry, joining a startup

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:21.720
and we also have alternative careers

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.513
so there's way more coming up.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.200
So we're gonna start to
ask you a first question

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:34.740
and I think we're gonna ask
that to Emily and Connor

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:37.120
what it's like to work
in a in a research group?

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:39.389
So you joined to go to grad school

00:09:39.389 --> 00:09:41.580
it started different than undergrads

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.960
because you don't really
join a school per se.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.130
You don't have that, it's
not a curriculum of classes.

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:47.840
You have some classes
for the masters degree

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.976
but you also join a research group,

00:09:50.976 --> 00:09:52.560
what it's like to work
in the research group

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:54.210
and how did you pick the groups?

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.240
And can you share your experience

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.983
on your day-to-day activities?

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:03.493
- I can go first.

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:06.810
So it's been a little strange for me

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:11.810
because when I joined the
group I'm currently in

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:15.150
like the next semester COVID hit

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:20.150
so a lot of it has been,
you know, remote over Zoom

00:10:20.740 --> 00:10:22.500
just like this panel

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:26.470
but in a way it's been really cool.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.360
So even though I haven't been able

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:32.420
to get to know everyone in my group,

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:33.570
because it is a large group

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:35.760
with like a lot of different interests.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.510
I've gotten to know some people very well

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:41.290
who are studying similar things to me

00:10:41.290 --> 00:10:44.660
but then I also get to
meet lots of professors

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:47.090
and students at other universities.

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:50.060
And so it's almost like
they're in my group

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.890
because we're all working
on the same stuff.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:55.300
And so like a standard week for me

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:59.860
would be like working with
professors at like UMass Campus

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:01.930
so MIT and NAU

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:06.420
because it's the Center for
Quantum Networks is really great

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.120
where they really like
emphasize collaboration

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:14.420
so that's really interesting.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:18.030
And so I enjoy getting to
work with these professors

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:20.160
and then other graduate students

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.683
and I even get to work with
undergrads, which is exciting.

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:30.340
And I think that as an
undergrad I was very intimidated

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:31.970
when I was working in a lab

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:36.210
I was like intimidated by my
superiors and now I realize

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:41.210
that like, we're all just like
on this pursuit to, you know,

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:42.643
further the field.

00:11:44.219 --> 00:11:48.560
And like people are going to
respect my ideas just as much

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:51.033
and like I actually am contributing.

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:55.143
So that's exciting.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:00.650
- Yeah, I can jump in

00:12:00.650 --> 00:12:03.670
and say a couple of things
about my experience as well.

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.250
Maybe I should qualify what I'm gonna say

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.253
by pointing out that of course,

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:12.610
different research groups

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:14.720
are gonna have worrying
different kinds of environments.

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.340
So a lot of what your
experience is gonna be like

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.370
is gonna depend on the
professor you might choose

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.157
to work with and how that
professor runs their group

00:12:20.157 --> 00:12:22.850
and what kind of atmosphere they foster.

00:12:22.850 --> 00:12:24.950
So to say a little bit
about my own experience,

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:27.710
I'm a theorist so I don't
do any experiments myself.

00:12:27.710 --> 00:12:29.040
I wouldn't be any good at it

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:32.450
but I like doing pen and paper
calculations and simulations.

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:33.877
And so what that means for me

00:12:33.877 --> 00:12:37.370
and my research group on
the day to day is, you know

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:39.650
maybe about two thirds of
my time in any given day

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:41.670
is devoted mostly to my own work

00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:43.650
either reading papers,
working out problems,

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:45.290
running simulations, this kind of thing

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:47.790
but, you know, there maybe
remaining one-third of every day

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:49.810
is usually collaborative work

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:52.340
either meetings or working out
problems with other people.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:53.830
And it's probably that portion of the day

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:55.150
that I think is the most fun.

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:55.990
Those will be the times

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:57.590
when I'll chat with my research advisor

00:12:57.590 --> 00:12:59.270
we'll try to brainstorm ways

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:01.230
to get around challenging
aspects of problems

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:02.810
or I'll work with other people in my group

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.200
and discuss topics that
we're all interested in

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.790
or attend seminars or do other things.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:09.180
I think similar to Emily,

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:13.180
one of the really nice
aspects about being in a PhD

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:15.090
and getting to study interesting problems

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.840
is that oftentimes it really
is a very collaborative effort.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.350
So not only do I have a number of people

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:21.930
within my own research group

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:23.510
that I'm working with at any given time

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:25.520
there are also other
people at other groups

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:28.680
within the university and
at other universities still.

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:32.820
So having a very nice and
collaborative research environment

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:34.710
has been a really valuable thing

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:35.747
that I've really appreciated

00:13:35.747 --> 00:13:40.103
and one aspect of my research
group that I've really liked.

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:44.270
Another thing, I think Florian
you mentioned how, you know

00:13:44.270 --> 00:13:46.710
people wind up choosing
different research advisors

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:48.910
one thing for me that
I think was really nice

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:52.000
as I shared with my potential advisors

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:53.970
I placed a premium on being able

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:57.170
to communicate effectively with my advisor

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.130
and really get a good sense for, you know

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:00.620
not only what they were interested in

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:01.870
but feel like we could have an easy

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:05.000
and free exchange of ideas
and that sort of thing

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:07.330
and that's been something really nice

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:09.250
that has helped me a lot in my PhD

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:11.730
feeling like I was always on
the same page as my advisor

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:14.240
we could work very well
and effectively together.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:15.950
And so that's something

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:17.890
about my own particular
research environment

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:19.340
that I've really appreciated.

00:14:20.775 --> 00:14:21.860
- Thank you both.

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:24.287
And to fill up on that the
other question in the chat

00:14:24.287 --> 00:14:28.180
"what led you to choose
your respective grad school?

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:30.860
What was the sort of like, sort of hinted

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:32.200
at like how you picked your groups,

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:35.000
but like why the different school

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:37.513
and like what made you go to grad school?

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:45.963
- Well, for one reason it was
one of the places I got in-

00:14:47.322 --> 00:14:49.489
- Its a very valid reason.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:55.470
- But I would say that
like the best thing about,

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:58.630
like I think for me, like going
to the University of Arizona

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:01.220
was the flexibility I was
given at the beginning.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:05.510
So I don't feel like I am behind

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:09.090
because I didn't go in knowing
which group I wanted to be in

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:11.610
and so that actually gave
me time to fill it out

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:15.470
and see who I thought
I would work well with.

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:18.680
So that was a really nice for me

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:23.680
but I do know others like
colleagues who kind of, you know

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:27.050
how to choose their group
before they went to grad school

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:30.660
and maybe then they've
realized that their advisors

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:34.160
are not everything they
were looking for in a mentor

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:36.930
but the nice thing is that
often even in those cases

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.020
I think that you can find someone else

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:42.163
like whether it be a
postdoc or like another,

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:44.730
because you're collaborating
with so many people

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:45.760
you can always find somebody

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:48.900
to help fill the needs that you have.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:52.270
But I did like how going
to UoA gave me the time

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:55.650
to like, in-person fill out groups

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:58.133
and see what research I wanted to do.

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:04.060
- Yeah, and in my own case, I actually

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:07.280
I wound up applying to a large
number of graduate programs

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:10.360
because I was applying
together with my girlfriend,

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:11.200
now fiance.

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:13.280
And we wanted to try to
go to the same program

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:14.920
so we tried to maximize our chances

00:16:14.920 --> 00:16:16.090
and apply in many places.

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:19.530
But, so I had a couple of
programs that I was looking at

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:20.610
and all of them

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:23.410
seem like they would have
great research opportunities,

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:24.450
cool things in quantum

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:28.480
so for me, a lot of the
decision between those programs

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:32.150
came down to some
non-academic factors too,

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:32.983
well, maybe first

00:16:32.983 --> 00:16:34.760
I should say with regard
to the academic factors

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:37.920
one sort of prerequisite
that I used as a filter

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:40.310
to try and determine what
a good programs might be

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:44.360
is not only did I want to be
able to identify a advisor

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:46.200
at each of the potential
candidate programs

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:47.290
someone who I had talked to

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:48.800
and thought I could work well with,

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:51.760
I wanted to make sure there
was more than one such advisor

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:53.182
at each of these places

00:16:53.182 --> 00:16:55.500
because you never know you
could go work someplace

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:56.900
and find out that, you know

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:58.570
that group isn't really
the way it appeared,

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:00.770
maybe it's not a good
fit for you after all

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:02.440
and you don't wanna be, you know

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:03.900
stuck out without a research group

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:05.410
where you feel like you
would be a good fit.

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:07.320
So having someplace

00:17:07.320 --> 00:17:08.870
where it seems like
there are a lot of groups

00:17:08.870 --> 00:17:10.090
that you could work nicely in

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:13.617
and see yourself thriving
and having a nice team time

00:17:13.617 --> 00:17:16.833
and at least for me was an
important factor in my decision.

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:22.440
Another thing that in my case
was kind of a deciding factor

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:25.230
for Yale was I thought it
was a really nice environment

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:26.580
among the graduate students.

00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:28.950
So in going and visiting the program

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:31.000
and getting to talk to the other students

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:33.660
everyone seemed by and large,

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.150
well, I should say physics grad school

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:36.900
can be a stressful time for sure,

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.160
but people seemed by and large happy

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:40.900
despite the stresses that are inevitable

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:41.970
in a graduate program.

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:45.170
And, you know, people
weren't massively overworked

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:46.760
they weren't working a hundred hour weeks

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:48.990
or anything crazy like this.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:50.580
You know, it seemed like an environment

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:53.550
where not only did people
go and learn physics

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:54.780
and do exciting research

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:56.510
but they also enjoyed it
while they were doing it.

00:17:56.510 --> 00:17:59.450
And there was a nice physics
graduate student community

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:01.190
that I wanted to be a part of.

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:05.250
So, you know, I guess
the takeaway from this

00:18:05.250 --> 00:18:07.190
being that, you know, it's totally fine

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:10.419
and I think important to consider
also non-academic factors

00:18:10.419 --> 00:18:12.420
when looking at a graduate program

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:14.730
thinking about the kind of
life you might have there

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:16.710
because of course, you know, five, six

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:20.170
or however many years it takes
to do a PhD is a long time

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:22.640
and you wanna think about
all the different things

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:24.310
that might contribute
to your quality of life

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:25.260
while you're there.

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:30.240
- Ann, do you want to
chime in on explaining

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:33.070
like how people can, like,
if you have general advice

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:34.550
on like how to bake your grad school,

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:37.460
like why they should choose
a certain grad school?

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:39.853
Like, what are the options for people

00:18:39.853 --> 00:18:42.300
and why they should choose
certain grad school?

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:43.920
What are the criteria

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:46.590
that basically grad students would choose

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:47.890
or should look forward to?

00:18:49.187 --> 00:18:51.420
- I think what you want to do

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:54.610
is first think about why you
wanna go to graduate school,

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:56.860
what do you want to accomplish?

00:18:56.860 --> 00:18:58.510
Do you need a graduate degree?

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:02.870
As Connor said, graduate
school, it's a long time

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.650
five years for many degrees,

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:08.770
sometimes six if you
encounter some roadblocks

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:13.070
or something, you need to switch advisors.

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:16.750
So you really wanna make sure
that it's the thing for you.

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:19.010
And what do you want to learn?

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:22.623
Because what are the questions
that really excite you?

00:19:23.751 --> 00:19:26.690
If, you know, quantum is interdisciplinary

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:31.690
so are there a number of
people or approaches to quantum

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:35.710
that you might want to be investigating?

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:37.640
I think Emily talked

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:42.250
about the fact that she's been
able to explore and, you know

00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:44.760
especially if you're coming
straight out of undergrad

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:47.580
you may not be a hundred percent sure

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:49.740
of what about quantum is appealing.

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:52.090
And so a place where there
are a number of people

00:19:52.090 --> 00:19:54.130
whose research looks interesting to you

00:19:54.130 --> 00:19:55.693
is gonna be very important.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:59.260
Another thing to be thinking about

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:03.300
is what do you hope to do
when you finish a degree?

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:05.270
And I'm not saying that to scare you

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:07.040
you shouldn't have your life planned out

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:12.040
but are you really interested
in pursuing research?

00:20:12.410 --> 00:20:16.140
Are you thinking about
maybe teaching high school?

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:19.130
Are you thinking that you
want to go work in industry

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:23.440
and different kinds of careers

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:27.600
often require a different kind of training

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:30.710
and so if you're thinking
of, you know, I love physics,

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:32.580
I like the idea of quantum

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:34.680
but I'm really thinking
I wanna teach high school

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.670
you may not need to get a PhD

00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:38.613
and a masters degree might be enough.

00:20:40.150 --> 00:20:41.840
What about funding?

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:45.010
You know, do I have, am
I independently wealthy?

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.083
Most of us are not.

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:51.650
But graduate school, the nice
thing about most PhD programs

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:54.030
is you don't pay for them.

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:58.710
They pay you to study,
conduct research and teach.

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:00.890
And that's something that
some students don't know

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:01.830
that masters degrees

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:05.540
you usually have to pay for out
of pocket or take out loans.

00:21:05.540 --> 00:21:10.540
But PhDs, usually if it's
a research institution

00:21:11.330 --> 00:21:13.633
of some repute,

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:18.860
it's through a combination
of teaching and research

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:21.700
you won't be paying for your degree.

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:24.160
And so that's something
to be thinking about too.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:25.630
And then, you know

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:27.180
I think the most important thing

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:28.550
is what kinds of questions

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:30.290
are the ones that you're
most interested in?

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:31.790
That'll help you

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:34.650
and your undergraduate advisors
should be able to help you

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:38.240
identify some programs
that might be a good fit

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:40.550
for your interests.

00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:44.470
You'll want to look up some of the faculty

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:46.150
at the places you're thinking of

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:49.210
and see what their specializations are.

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:52.930
And I'm sure that Connor and
Emily can weigh in on this

00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:54.890
that, you know, as Connor said

00:21:54.890 --> 00:21:57.310
you don't wanna put all
your eggs in one basket

00:21:57.310 --> 00:21:59.930
you wanna make sure that there's as...

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:02.540
because research interests do evolve

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:05.400
are there two or three people
you might be interested

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:06.830
in working with?

00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:08.740
I see in the chat that there's a question

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:11.020
about "how do you reach out to advisors

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:15.530
at programs you're not yet
accepted in or involved in"?

00:22:15.530 --> 00:22:17.160
And one place to start

00:22:17.160 --> 00:22:19.820
would be the Director of Graduate Studies

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:22.570
or the Director of Graduate Admissions.

00:22:22.570 --> 00:22:27.110
Every program has at least a
Director of Graduate Studies

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:28.920
some programs, because
they're large enough

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:30.760
have a Director of Graduate Admissions

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:32.640
and so you could say to them

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:34.770
I'm really interested in your program,

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:36.820
these are the things that are interesting

00:22:37.997 --> 00:22:42.440
and I'd love to talk to
professor X or professor Y

00:22:44.219 --> 00:22:47.270
or I'm really interested in X and Y

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:49.770
I'm not sure which of your faculty

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:52.480
are the ones that I might want to contact

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:54.823
but can you tell me about your program?

00:22:55.860 --> 00:23:00.350
So that's one way to start that process.

00:23:00.350 --> 00:23:03.460
If you've read something of
someone's or you have heard

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:07.130
or your undergraduate
advisor says to you, you know

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:08.020
given your interest,

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:11.120
you might be interested in
going to the university of X

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:13.520
and working with professor Y,

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:16.720
then you can just write an
email to professor Y and say

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:21.490
dear professor Y, my
undergraduate advisor,

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:24.787
I mean, I've been getting
a degree in physics

00:23:24.787 --> 00:23:29.480
or computer science or engineering

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:33.670
and I'm interested in graduate school,

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:38.070
my advisor told me that your
research is very much in line

00:23:38.070 --> 00:23:40.860
with the things I'm
interested in exploring.

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:44.220
Would you have some time to speak to me?

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:46.630
And now we can do that virtually,

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:49.130
if you're local, you
wanna try and go visit

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:50.530
so you could actually meet them.

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:51.700
I think Emily's point

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:56.140
about understanding the
community you're trying to join

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:57.343
is really important.

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:02.090
But you really wanna
be doing your homework

00:24:02.090 --> 00:24:03.560
so that you can figure out

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:06.340
what are the places
that might be a good fit

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:08.243
for what you want to accomplish.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:12.320
- And once you pick your grad school

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:13.870
I know that Yale has that

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:16.305
and I'm sure University
of Arizona has it too

00:24:16.305 --> 00:24:18.470
there's something called
rotation or circulation

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:23.090
and so you pick a group for one
semester and sort of a trial

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:25.180
Connor and Emily, have you done this?

00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:27.420
Have you taken the opportunity to do this?

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:29.920
And did you switch, did you
stay with the professor?

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:33.160
What were your experiences with that?

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:37.030
- Yeah, so I switched around
the first three semesters

00:24:37.030 --> 00:24:37.947
at the UoA.

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:43.260
You can take independent
studies alongside your classes

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:47.289
and so like, that's common
is that you take two classes

00:24:47.289 --> 00:24:49.457
and you're independent study

00:24:49.457 --> 00:24:51.370
up until you have like your masters

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:52.730
and that independent study

00:24:52.730 --> 00:24:56.970
will just be you working
with a different group.

00:24:56.970 --> 00:25:01.970
And here it was nice

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:05.980
because you're getting funded
through your TA-ing initially.

00:25:05.980 --> 00:25:10.170
So if you are not enjoying one
of the groups that you're in

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:12.380
it doesn't put a ton of pressure on you

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:15.910
to like have to get certain
results and things like that

00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:19.760
for like any funding purposes.

00:25:19.760 --> 00:25:24.760
But yeah, so I started off in
like a nuclear theory group

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.630
and then the next semester
I went into a biophysics lab

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:34.690
and then I realized, nope I
would much rather be a theorist.

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:37.500
So then I went back to what I knew I loved

00:25:37.500 --> 00:25:40.480
and I joined the quantum
photonics applications group

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:42.150
which it was nice

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:46.480
and I chose it because they
have experimentalists in it

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:49.760
and they have theorists and
they have everything in between.

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:53.710
So I figured I could probably
just spend the rest of my time

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:56.070
in this group and try
out everything I want

00:25:56.070 --> 00:25:57.707
to my heart's content (chuckles).

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:01.810
- Yeah, I'll just chime in and quickly add

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:04.240
that I think I had a pretty
similar experience to Emily

00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:06.484
at least within the physics
department here at Yale

00:26:06.484 --> 00:26:09.220
there is an informal rotation system

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:11.710
and so what I did was during
my first semester here

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:13.560
I actually just sort of worked informally

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:14.770
in a couple of groups

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:17.050
mainly just kind of
doing informal readings,

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:18.270
seeing what they were working on,

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:20.203
getting a sense for what
the research was like

00:26:20.203 --> 00:26:21.680
and through that process,

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.840
I got a sense of maybe
which of the two groups

00:26:23.840 --> 00:26:26.370
I was looking at seemed like
it would be a better fit for me

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:31.010
and then I started doing some
very, very basic low, you know

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:32.630
low time commitment research with them

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:34.880
during my first academic year.

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:37.807
Here at Yale it's, you know,
the first two years you teach

00:26:37.807 --> 00:26:40.180
and so that's what supports
you during the academic years.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:43.070
And so similar to, as
Emily said, then it's nice

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:44.320
because you don't have
to have the pressure

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:45.880
to be funded by your research group

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:48.160
during those first two academic years.

00:26:48.160 --> 00:26:50.390
So you can, you know, put in
as much time as you're able

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:52.460
while also focusing on
teaching and classes.

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:54.230
And so it gives you a
little bit of a period

00:26:54.230 --> 00:26:56.710
to try out some different research groups.

00:26:56.710 --> 00:26:58.880
I should say that not all
programs work this way,

00:26:58.880 --> 00:26:59.730
I think there are some

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:01.940
where you're funded
directly by research groups

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:04.430
or you can be as soon as you
sort of set foot in the program

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:06.730
so, you know, looking
at different programs

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:08.626
and trying to understand
the different funding models

00:27:08.626 --> 00:27:10.850
is actually something
that can be important

00:27:10.850 --> 00:27:11.683
and relevant to you

00:27:11.683 --> 00:27:14.050
as someone potentially
considering graduate school

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:17.280
'cause that can definitely have
an impact on your experience

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:20.290
or more generally just the
the flexibility you might have

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:21.790
in exploring different groups.

00:27:23.090 --> 00:27:24.590
- Yes, funding is the name of the game

00:27:24.590 --> 00:27:26.900
and I'm gonna flash on my screen.

00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:29.460
If you go on any of the
website of the grad school

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:31.470
usually you have a tab with the resources

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:32.630
and the first thing that pops in

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:34.010
is the funding and the grants

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:37.286
because, of course, you
need to get your fundings

00:27:37.286 --> 00:27:39.520
there's no need of going to grad school

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:41.400
if it's to just crushing bone (laughing)

00:27:41.400 --> 00:27:43.730
and then not having any money.

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:47.060
And one thing that's often
haze is the teaching.

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:48.810
You talk about, both of
them talked about that

00:27:48.810 --> 00:27:50.610
then we have a question in the chat.

00:27:51.510 --> 00:27:53.200
Somebody is interested
in hearing your thoughts

00:27:53.200 --> 00:27:56.190
on the teaching side, how
much time does it take?

00:27:56.190 --> 00:27:58.010
And do you wish it was more teaching,

00:27:58.010 --> 00:28:00.470
less teaching, what's the balance is it...

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:04.290
Yeah, and so the person said that

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:06.510
teaching is not something
that he particularly enjoy,

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:08.510
oh, sorry, they particularly enjoy

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:11.330
and they are worried
it's gonna be a burden

00:28:11.330 --> 00:28:14.226
like, can you give us the
the ratio of time to spend

00:28:14.226 --> 00:28:16.476
preparing class and TA-ing?

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:21.210
- I think that really
depends on both the class

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:23.773
your TA-ing and where you're doing it.

00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:27.920
I know that at the UoA you can be a grader

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:30.840
so if you don't enjoy
standing up in front of people

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:35.480
or like maybe you don't wanna
have those like interactions

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:38.090
like there is the option to be a grader

00:28:38.090 --> 00:28:40.970
in which case you'd
never actually interact

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:44.440
with the students you just
interact with the professors.

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:48.260
That however is my least
favorite part of teaching

00:28:48.260 --> 00:28:50.900
and I also saw I'm a slow grader

00:28:50.900 --> 00:28:53.430
and I don't find it
rewarding at all (chuckles).

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:58.430
So I much prefer when I'm
teaching labs and discussions

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:03.330
but I know that in the
physics department at the UoA,

00:29:03.330 --> 00:29:04.610
the time commitment

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:08.570
it varies so much depending on the class

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:11.980
because it'll be like, if
you're doing discussions

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:14.950
maybe you only really need to be there

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:16.220
for the discussion times

00:29:16.220 --> 00:29:19.580
but then there's not much to
grade or prepare ahead of time.

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:21.020
And then, but if you're doing labs

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:22.830
you may have to like help set up,

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:25.270
and then grade the lab
reports or grade tests.

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:29.520
So I know that they emphasize

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:33.720
that you shouldn't be spending
more than 20 hours a week

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:38.350
on TA-ing because that's all
you're really getting paid for

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:41.980
is you get paid to be a part-time teacher

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:44.940
and then other part-time
you're this being a student

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:49.460
but I do know that it can be difficult

00:29:49.460 --> 00:29:50.983
for some people to balance.

00:29:53.150 --> 00:29:55.750
I would recommend that if
you get into your program

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:57.230
perhaps talk to some of the students there

00:29:57.230 --> 00:29:59.690
and they'll have an idea of
like what are the classes

00:29:59.690 --> 00:30:03.160
that would work best for you.

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:05.170
But I'll say, I really love TA-ing

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:07.840
because I always went to grad school

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:10.240
for like physics education research.

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:14.760
So now I'm funded through my research

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:15.900
so I don't need to TA,

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:18.260
but instead I'm trying to come up

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:20.690
with a nice outreach opportunities

00:30:21.860 --> 00:30:25.483
so that I could still fulfill
that, like part of myself.

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:29.275
- Ann you were trying to say something?

00:30:29.275 --> 00:30:30.108
- I was just gonna say

00:30:30.108 --> 00:30:33.550
that it really depends both
the amount of teachings

00:30:33.550 --> 00:30:37.140
whether it's 20 hours a
week or 10 hours a week

00:30:37.140 --> 00:30:41.120
the kinds of courses, whether
you're leading a section

00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:42.240
or whether you're doing grading

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:46.000
really will depend on the
school and the research group.

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:47.320
And so, you know

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:52.320
physics students may be asked
to teach a different amount

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:55.520
than computer science
students say at Yale.

00:30:55.520 --> 00:30:58.490
So there's a great deal of variety.

00:30:58.490 --> 00:31:02.630
I think one thing to know
is that at least at Yale

00:31:02.630 --> 00:31:06.830
we have a training program
for first-time teachers.

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:09.150
So people who may say, oh my God

00:31:09.150 --> 00:31:11.767
I don't know how, I can't
stand in front of a classroom,

00:31:11.767 --> 00:31:14.990
I don't, you know, that isn't me,

00:31:14.990 --> 00:31:18.610
there are a lot of tools to
help you become a better teacher

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:22.420
but I'd also say it also, you know,

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:25.510
depending upon what program you enter

00:31:25.510 --> 00:31:27.770
some programs won't
allow students to teach

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:28.850
in their first year.

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:31.470
Some students start teaching
their very first year.

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:36.300
Really what you want to do
is, you know, do your homework

00:31:36.300 --> 00:31:38.540
the nice thing is everything's on the web.

00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:41.570
You can usually find out
when you go to the department

00:31:41.570 --> 00:31:43.330
that you're thinking of joining

00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:48.330
when the teaching is and at
what level and if they have,

00:31:48.450 --> 00:31:52.540
usually you're not just
thrown out into the classroom

00:31:52.540 --> 00:31:54.470
and asked to sink or swim,

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:55.670
usually there are people there

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:58.270
to help you learn how to do this well.

00:31:58.270 --> 00:31:59.990
And you may find

00:31:59.990 --> 00:32:02.110
that it's something that
you didn't think you liked

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:04.630
but then there's a particular
aspect you really like,

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:09.233
as Emily said that she loves
the interpersonal aspect,

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:11.740
but doesn't like the grading.

00:32:11.740 --> 00:32:14.460
And you might find that
actually you find the grading

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:15.720
or even just being there

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:20.720
sometimes TAs are there
to help answer questions.

00:32:20.930 --> 00:32:25.830
You know, sometimes you're
not actually leading a section

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:26.663
you may find

00:32:26.663 --> 00:32:29.460
that one-on-one work is
something that you really enjoy.

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:32.280
So I think there are
various forms of teaching

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:35.460
and whatever program

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:37.630
and institution you're thinking of joining

00:32:37.630 --> 00:32:38.870
you'd wanna ask about that

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:39.703
what does it look like

00:32:39.703 --> 00:32:42.610
and what are the different
kinds of opportunities

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:43.623
available to you?

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:48.340
- It looks like somebody
asked if you're a TA

00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:51.130
do you need to develop your
own curriculum for the class?

00:32:51.130 --> 00:32:55.090
So I would say that's unlikely

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:58.280
I know at the UoA there's opportunities

00:32:58.280 --> 00:33:02.620
to be at like a summer
course teacher, in which case

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:07.250
I assume you have more like
control over the curriculum

00:33:07.250 --> 00:33:09.490
but I know that in my experience

00:33:09.490 --> 00:33:12.881
it's been like the
worksheets and the lab books

00:33:12.881 --> 00:33:15.910
and the labs and everything
is already set up

00:33:15.910 --> 00:33:18.280
and then you'd mainly just like alter it

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:21.950
so that it like works for
your students in the room.

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:24.010
So like you would give an introduction

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:25.950
based off of what you think they know

00:33:25.950 --> 00:33:29.530
or what they've learned from
you in the previous labs

00:33:29.530 --> 00:33:32.830
and then maybe you realize
that you have a very slow group

00:33:32.830 --> 00:33:35.730
so you might like take certain parts out

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:39.740
that are less important,
but that was my experience

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:43.320
so I think that's less that
you create the curriculum

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:47.363
and more of you to just kind
like apply it to the students.

00:33:48.250 --> 00:33:49.083
- I would add

00:33:49.083 --> 00:33:50.880
that I think that's
probably pretty typical.

00:33:50.880 --> 00:33:52.020
I think most programs

00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:54.040
know that not everyone
may be as interested

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:55.470
in doing a whole lot of teaching

00:33:55.470 --> 00:33:56.830
or developing their own materials

00:33:56.830 --> 00:33:59.280
and so they probably try to
minimize the amount of time

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:00.520
you would have to do that.

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:02.840
Certainly here at Yale, most
of the teaching assignments

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:05.270
are exactly the kind of
thing Emily described

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:07.610
but I will say on the flip side of that

00:34:07.610 --> 00:34:09.870
if you're someone who is
interested in teaching,

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:11.930
I happen to also be very
interested in teaching

00:34:11.930 --> 00:34:13.650
and I've tried to really
make it an important part

00:34:13.650 --> 00:34:16.100
of my graduate school experience,

00:34:16.100 --> 00:34:19.350
opportunities too, for instance,
design your own curriculum

00:34:19.350 --> 00:34:20.800
can also be very exciting things

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:22.550
so those are some things I've sought out

00:34:22.550 --> 00:34:24.630
and actually one aspect for me

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:26.920
of my graduate experience
that I've really enjoyed

00:34:26.920 --> 00:34:28.140
is being in an environment

00:34:28.140 --> 00:34:31.020
where there are a lot of
resources surrounding teaching

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:33.420
so you can get involved in
the teaching community here

00:34:33.420 --> 00:34:34.390
more broadly,

00:34:34.390 --> 00:34:37.800
learn about how to be a better
and more effective teacher.

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:40.560
And so, you know, whether
teaching is a plus

00:34:40.560 --> 00:34:41.393
or minus for you,

00:34:41.393 --> 00:34:43.820
I think it's certainly something
that's worth considering

00:34:43.820 --> 00:34:45.160
when looking at different programs

00:34:45.160 --> 00:34:46.300
and important to bear in mind

00:34:46.300 --> 00:34:47.670
all of the different ways

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:49.170
that it might affect your experience.

00:34:49.170 --> 00:34:50.800
For me, it's been a huge positive here.

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:53.630
I've joined Yale Center
for teaching and learning

00:34:53.630 --> 00:34:56.410
helping to teach other new
graduate students how to teach

00:34:56.410 --> 00:34:57.860
that's all something I've really enjoyed

00:34:57.860 --> 00:35:00.340
but for other people
who don't like teaching

00:35:00.340 --> 00:35:02.980
Yale's also been a nice
option as an example

00:35:02.980 --> 00:35:05.180
because the required number
of teaching hours per week

00:35:05.180 --> 00:35:07.060
is only 10 within the first two years.

00:35:07.060 --> 00:35:09.340
So comparatively, I think
that's a fairly small number

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:11.860
but you know, all sorts
of different programs

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:13.810
are gonna have different pros and cons.

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:15.850
It's really gonna come
down to doing your research

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:17.950
and seeing how the particular program

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:19.400
might fit with your interest.

00:35:20.993 --> 00:35:22.610
- [Florian] Go a head Russ

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:24.190
- I was just gonna chime in for a second.

00:35:24.190 --> 00:35:27.030
So even though my background
is not related to quantum,

00:35:27.030 --> 00:35:29.130
you know, going through grad school

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:32.040
I did a lot of teaching
of lab sections in geology

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:35.080
since that's what my degree
is in, and I loved it.

00:35:35.080 --> 00:35:37.480
I loved teaching as a graduate student.

00:35:37.480 --> 00:35:41.160
And I'm kind of speaking to the question,

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:43.050
developing your own curriculum

00:35:43.050 --> 00:35:45.820
one of the ways that I actually got funded

00:35:45.820 --> 00:35:49.350
was by helping develop the lab curriculum.

00:35:49.350 --> 00:35:50.980
So it'll probably be
a little bit different

00:35:50.980 --> 00:35:52.160
in engineering and physics

00:35:52.160 --> 00:35:53.920
but there are those types of opportunities

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:57.650
where you can definitely
kind of shape the curriculum

00:35:57.650 --> 00:35:59.640
if that's something you're interested in

00:35:59.640 --> 00:36:01.970
but generally you're not expected to

00:36:01.970 --> 00:36:03.483
just from my experience so.

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:05.863
- All right, that's good.

00:36:05.863 --> 00:36:06.796
Thank you all.

00:36:06.796 --> 00:36:07.966
So we have more-
- [Emily] (indistinct)

00:36:07.966 --> 00:36:08.799
- Oh, go ahead.

00:36:08.799 --> 00:36:09.632
Do you want to say something more?

00:36:09.632 --> 00:36:11.790
- Sorry, I saw that something said

00:36:11.790 --> 00:36:13.280
like, if you do enjoy teaching,

00:36:13.280 --> 00:36:15.198
like what should you look for in a school

00:36:15.198 --> 00:36:18.370
I was gonna say that if
you see that a school

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:19.870
has like a professor

00:36:19.870 --> 00:36:22.594
who focuses on like
physics education research

00:36:22.594 --> 00:36:24.720
or something like that,

00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:29.720
that's probably going to be
like incentivize going there

00:36:29.720 --> 00:36:30.553
because like you know

00:36:30.553 --> 00:36:32.410
that they're gonna be
keeping up with research

00:36:32.410 --> 00:36:33.620
and they're going to be like willing

00:36:33.620 --> 00:36:38.300
to like actually study what's
going on in the classroom

00:36:38.300 --> 00:36:40.590
whereas other schools

00:36:40.590 --> 00:36:42.760
may just kind of have outdated materials

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:45.960
which I know can become
frustrating to have to teach

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:47.433
even if you enjoy teaching.

00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:50.520
- One other quick detail on
this point, I just mentioned,

00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:52.560
as many schools will also have centers

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:53.540
for teaching and learning

00:36:53.540 --> 00:36:55.120
which could be a good
contact point for you.

00:36:55.120 --> 00:36:56.770
If you are interested in teaching

00:36:56.770 --> 00:36:58.560
they could tell you what
the experience is like

00:36:58.560 --> 00:36:59.690
and resources you could find

00:36:59.690 --> 00:37:02.050
if you wanted to further
explore that interest

00:37:02.050 --> 00:37:03.940
but that's not a thing that's
present at all schools.

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:05.060
So if you are interested, you know

00:37:05.060 --> 00:37:06.000
that would be a good sign

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:07.800
and a place you could find out more.

00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:11.106
- And also, depending on what
you're gonna do with your PhD,

00:37:11.106 --> 00:37:12.250
if you want to join industry,

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:14.160
teaching is not gonna be as important

00:37:14.160 --> 00:37:17.410
as if you want to become a
professor and have your own group

00:37:17.410 --> 00:37:20.160
because you would be
required to create curriculum

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:21.070
and so it really depends

00:37:21.070 --> 00:37:23.780
on what's your career goal at the end.

00:37:23.780 --> 00:37:25.890
So it's something that you might want...

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:29.130
I know that five to six, seven
years is like a long time

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:31.670
but you really need to come in with a plan

00:37:31.670 --> 00:37:35.300
because you might struggle
to find the motivation

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:36.360
because it's a very long time

00:37:36.360 --> 00:37:38.550
if you sort of don't know
what's the end goal is,

00:37:38.550 --> 00:37:41.350
you should not really join a PhD program

00:37:41.350 --> 00:37:44.590
just because you get to be
called a doctor at the end,

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:48.450
which is great, we love it,
but it's a lot of struggle.

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:50.160
And there's a ton of question in the chat

00:37:50.160 --> 00:37:51.920
about stress and struggle

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:53.640
and also a lot about the funding.

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:55.410
Maybe we're gonna go
with the stress one first

00:37:55.410 --> 00:37:58.068
so we don't end up on a note too bleak,

00:37:58.068 --> 00:38:01.288
but it's true that a lot of grad students

00:38:01.288 --> 00:38:06.288
have period of stress, some
difficult, some depression.

00:38:06.381 --> 00:38:10.200
Is there any tools that the
grad school has in place

00:38:10.200 --> 00:38:12.182
to help these kind of students?

00:38:12.182 --> 00:38:14.780
- Sure, I'm happy to take that.

00:38:14.780 --> 00:38:16.523
And then if Connor and Emily

00:38:16.523 --> 00:38:19.970
want to speak from their
personal experience.

00:38:19.970 --> 00:38:24.060
I think graduate school
is the best of times

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:25.720
and the worst of times (chuckles)

00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:29.130
and I think it's exciting

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:31.850
because you're focusing

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:33.690
on exactly what you wanna think about

00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:35.140
and spend your time thinking about

00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:37.210
and you're with like-minded people

00:38:37.210 --> 00:38:38.840
but there's a lot of pressure

00:38:38.840 --> 00:38:42.150
to get things done in a timely fashion

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:45.124
research, sometimes hits a dead end,

00:38:45.124 --> 00:38:48.500
there may be problems with an advisor

00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:52.260
and then, you know all
kinds of life can interfere.

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:53.800
You can get sick.

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:57.990
I think when you're
looking at graduate schools

00:38:57.990 --> 00:39:00.290
first of all, there are
two things to consider.

00:39:00.290 --> 00:39:03.720
One is you should always feel

00:39:03.720 --> 00:39:07.280
as though part of your
education is asking for help.

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:09.370
No one knows how to do graduate school

00:39:09.370 --> 00:39:10.640
until they've done it.

00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:13.110
So (chuckles) you just ask questions

00:39:13.110 --> 00:39:16.850
there's no such thing as
a bad or a stupid question

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:19.670
and you could be asking them of faculty,

00:39:19.670 --> 00:39:22.520
you can be asking them of your co-students

00:39:22.520 --> 00:39:25.690
so there may be an older
student that you get to know

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:29.160
but never be afraid to say

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:32.150
I don't know where to go to do X

00:39:32.150 --> 00:39:36.020
or I'm interested in X where
can I learn more about it?

00:39:36.020 --> 00:39:40.900
Or I'm really struggling with
Y do you have any suggestions

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:43.980
about what I might do
or whom I might talk to?

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:47.040
And I can't speak to every institution

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:50.850
but I would say most institutions

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:53.380
have an office of student's life

00:39:53.380 --> 00:39:55.270
who will help you think about, you know

00:39:55.270 --> 00:39:58.200
how you bring together the intellectual

00:39:58.200 --> 00:40:01.760
and the personal side of, you know

00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:06.003
meeting people, issues with
housing, those kinds of things.

00:40:06.937 --> 00:40:11.060
Many schools have really
amped up their mental health

00:40:11.060 --> 00:40:14.780
and counseling and I think
stress is a big thing

00:40:14.780 --> 00:40:16.570
but there're also wellness centers.

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:20.470
And so I think having a good sense

00:40:20.470 --> 00:40:23.981
of when things are not going right for you

00:40:23.981 --> 00:40:27.650
and not being shy about asking for help

00:40:27.650 --> 00:40:31.940
and seeking counseling if you need it,

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:33.610
there's career services,

00:40:33.610 --> 00:40:35.410
there's the center for
teaching and learning,

00:40:35.410 --> 00:40:37.150
as Connor mentioned

00:40:37.150 --> 00:40:39.650
there's a writing center at most places

00:40:39.650 --> 00:40:41.400
so if you're struggling, you know,

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:44.470
to really put your ideas down on paper

00:40:44.470 --> 00:40:46.133
that's a place to go.

00:40:47.190 --> 00:40:48.023
And, you know

00:40:48.023 --> 00:40:50.670
struggle can take any
number of different forms.

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:54.560
What I would say is almost every student

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:56.950
will have some challenge that they face

00:40:56.950 --> 00:41:00.400
in the four to six years that
they're in graduate school.

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:03.570
And that's not a sign that
something's wrong with you

00:41:03.570 --> 00:41:05.870
that's just a sign that it's hard.

00:41:05.870 --> 00:41:10.400
And I think that your job
when you encounter a challenge

00:41:10.400 --> 00:41:12.750
is to find the right resources

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:16.860
and if you don't know, just
ask as many people as you can

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:18.330
your Director of Graduate Studies

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:20.360
should be able to help you with this.

00:41:20.360 --> 00:41:22.700
The Dean's office should be
able to help you with this.

00:41:22.700 --> 00:41:25.960
So, you know, if you
don't know where to start

00:41:25.960 --> 00:41:29.660
those are often good places to start.

00:41:29.660 --> 00:41:32.650
There's no sort of magic formula,
but I think the main thing

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:34.150
is to know yourself

00:41:34.150 --> 00:41:38.610
and know when you can't
handle something on your own.

00:41:38.610 --> 00:41:43.610
I think graduate school is set
up for people to ask for help

00:41:44.290 --> 00:41:46.100
that's why we have all these other offices

00:41:46.100 --> 00:41:49.070
and no one expects students
to just magically know

00:41:49.070 --> 00:41:50.170
how to do this.

00:41:50.170 --> 00:41:51.760
You don't know how to write a dissertation

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:54.170
until you've written a
dissertation (laughing).

00:41:54.170 --> 00:41:57.700
So I think the main thing is not to be shy

00:41:57.700 --> 00:41:59.440
I would say the most successful students

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:01.650
take advantage of all the resources

00:42:01.650 --> 00:42:03.570
that are available to them.

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:07.590
And I'm gonna stop there and
let Connor and Emily jump in.

00:42:07.590 --> 00:42:08.423
- Before you do that

00:42:08.423 --> 00:42:11.280
I also want to point out that
I've been through grad school

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:13.670
and that the pattern that I've seen so

00:42:13.670 --> 00:42:16.171
is that you start very excited about it.

00:42:16.171 --> 00:42:18.490
You're like you're brand
new students, it's very nice

00:42:18.490 --> 00:42:20.240
and then your advisor can help you

00:42:20.240 --> 00:42:22.240
because he has all the answer for now

00:42:22.240 --> 00:42:24.600
because you still learn about the field.

00:42:24.600 --> 00:42:25.910
And then you slowly realize

00:42:25.910 --> 00:42:27.760
that you're gonna start
to research a topic

00:42:27.760 --> 00:42:29.690
that you are the only one

00:42:29.690 --> 00:42:32.890
or like one of the few in
the world who know the answer

00:42:32.890 --> 00:42:36.790
and no Googling, no book,
no anything will help you

00:42:36.790 --> 00:42:39.860
because you are like on
the verge of knowledge

00:42:39.860 --> 00:42:41.740
and you're discovering new things.

00:42:41.740 --> 00:42:44.070
And it's also a very distressing time

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:46.440
because you feel that you're on your own

00:42:46.440 --> 00:42:49.530
and then this is what you need the help

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:52.230
of all your colleagues but also,

00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:54.210
you need to sort of internalize

00:42:54.210 --> 00:42:56.370
the fact that it's a hard job

00:42:56.370 --> 00:42:58.470
and then you're gonna go through

00:42:58.470 --> 00:43:02.060
this is not too difficult,
this is pretty good.

00:43:02.060 --> 00:43:03.550
And one thing that really helped me

00:43:03.550 --> 00:43:06.920
is to find a mentor that can help you.

00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:10.500
Often, I realized that
the admin, so not the PI

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:13.300
but like people helping
either some associate Dean,

00:43:13.300 --> 00:43:16.982
some manager in centers
and people like this

00:43:16.982 --> 00:43:18.320
working with students

00:43:18.320 --> 00:43:20.900
and doing programmings
are usually very helpful.

00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:22.470
When I was doing my PhD in France,

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:26.443
we had one of the admin who
was sort of like my confidant

00:43:26.443 --> 00:43:30.540
when something went wrong I
just went and ran onto him

00:43:30.540 --> 00:43:32.930
and it was just talking
about it, really helped.

00:43:32.930 --> 00:43:33.763
So that's something

00:43:33.763 --> 00:43:36.220
that you should really
consider looking for mental

00:43:36.220 --> 00:43:38.183
and asking for help, like Ann said.

00:43:39.570 --> 00:43:40.547
Go on Emily

00:43:40.547 --> 00:43:43.630
how stressful are you doing PhD (chuckles)

00:43:43.630 --> 00:43:44.585
I at least could know,

00:43:44.585 --> 00:43:46.700
now that you're don't, you
should look back and be like,

00:43:46.700 --> 00:43:47.533
oh, that's nice

00:43:47.533 --> 00:43:49.947
and Emily, I hope you're not
too stressed yet (laughing).

00:43:51.370 --> 00:43:55.440
- For me, it definitely
like oscillates with stress.

00:43:55.440 --> 00:43:58.160
Like I've been more
stressed out in grad school

00:43:58.160 --> 00:43:59.900
than I've ever been before

00:43:59.900 --> 00:44:04.540
but then also there's
just been lots of external

00:44:04.540 --> 00:44:08.340
to grad school stressors that
are coinciding with, you know

00:44:08.340 --> 00:44:12.050
trying to get my masters
and continuing the program

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:12.883
and things

00:44:12.883 --> 00:44:16.550
so you just have to
like try to balance that

00:44:16.550 --> 00:44:20.260
like, yes you want to get your PhD

00:44:20.260 --> 00:44:25.260
but you also need to like,
live a balanced, healthy life.

00:44:25.580 --> 00:44:28.670
And like the people,
you know, your advisors

00:44:28.670 --> 00:44:32.280
and your peers and stuff
should be rooting for you

00:44:32.280 --> 00:44:36.030
so do what you need to do to survive.

00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:41.530
But there are certain like stressors

00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:45.730
that I've seen 'cause like friends of mine

00:44:45.730 --> 00:44:48.940
to choose to not to
continue with their PhD

00:44:48.940 --> 00:44:50.810
and instead bust out of the program

00:44:50.810 --> 00:44:54.060
and so I think those are
important to be aware of

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:56.520
so that you can maybe avoid them.

00:44:56.520 --> 00:45:00.590
So some friends of mine maybe
would lose their funding

00:45:01.700 --> 00:45:03.700
and that was the only group

00:45:03.700 --> 00:45:05.500
that they were interested in being in.

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:10.500
And so like, it's hard to
do a lot of serious research

00:45:10.780 --> 00:45:13.250
and TA and do everything, you know

00:45:13.250 --> 00:45:17.410
all at the same time when
you're not getting funding

00:45:17.410 --> 00:45:19.810
for all the research work you're doing.

00:45:19.810 --> 00:45:23.010
So that's one thing is
like, it is good to know.

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:25.630
And I think it is okay to ask professors

00:45:25.630 --> 00:45:29.500
if they have funding for
you, like ahead of time.

00:45:29.500 --> 00:45:31.430
And like how many students

00:45:31.430 --> 00:45:35.790
may be they're expecting
to take on in the future.

00:45:35.790 --> 00:45:40.240
And then also asking
other graduate students

00:45:41.940 --> 00:45:43.590
in the group questions like this.

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:46.450
So if you know there's a professor
you wanna work with, like

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:47.770
you can maybe ask them,

00:45:47.770 --> 00:45:50.910
or you just directly email
students in the group

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:53.810
to see if they believe
that there's funding

00:45:53.810 --> 00:45:57.020
but also if it's like a
pleasant work experience

00:45:57.020 --> 00:46:00.000
because there's lots of really
talented people out there

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:02.950
who push their graduate students very hard

00:46:02.950 --> 00:46:05.480
and they have very high expectations

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:08.800
and maybe that's not
something that you want

00:46:09.797 --> 00:46:12.840
or like can withstand

00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:15.700
because you are doing
other things in your life

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:16.710
and you are taking classes

00:46:16.710 --> 00:46:18.593
and there are other responsibilities.

00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:22.763
And then also just being aware of,

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:28.080
not only like the department
that you're going to be joining

00:46:28.080 --> 00:46:31.920
but like the general area of your program

00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:35.810
so like I've had friends
who moved here to Tucson

00:46:35.810 --> 00:46:38.970
who then realized that
the Jewish community

00:46:38.970 --> 00:46:41.770
isn't quite as large
as they'd like it to be

00:46:41.770 --> 00:46:44.160
and that made their life very difficult.

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:45.580
And you know, maybe the UoA

00:46:45.580 --> 00:46:50.420
doesn't take off certain
holidays that they do take off

00:46:50.420 --> 00:46:54.240
so then they're not able
to like have all the tools

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:58.180
they think they need to
be happy on their own

00:46:58.180 --> 00:47:00.860
let alone then to have
to endure the stresses

00:47:00.860 --> 00:47:02.760
of the PhD program.

00:47:02.760 --> 00:47:05.680
So like, it is important,

00:47:05.680 --> 00:47:08.550
I'm not saying you should choose a school

00:47:08.550 --> 00:47:10.360
based only off of the area it's in

00:47:10.360 --> 00:47:13.160
but that can be an important factor

00:47:13.160 --> 00:47:16.510
and if you are going to make
it through your program.

00:47:16.510 --> 00:47:20.440
- I think the issues
that I'm hearing from you

00:47:20.440 --> 00:47:25.210
and I think Connor raised
earlier are community.

00:47:25.210 --> 00:47:27.810
So making sure you have
the kinds of communities

00:47:27.810 --> 00:47:28.980
you're gonna need to thrive.

00:47:28.980 --> 00:47:33.980
So if you're someone who's
very religious, you know

00:47:36.960 --> 00:47:39.610
make sure that there's
a religious community

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:42.160
that is either on campus or off campus

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:44.140
that is gonna really be supporting you

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:47.090
during these years.

00:47:47.090 --> 00:47:49.956
And communication which
Connor brought up earlier,

00:47:49.956 --> 00:47:53.110
really trying to suss out

00:47:53.110 --> 00:47:58.110
whether you are gonna get
on well with your advisor

00:47:58.430 --> 00:48:02.790
and that you're gonna be able
to talk directly with them

00:48:02.790 --> 00:48:05.710
about anything that arises

00:48:05.710 --> 00:48:07.890
and whether it's a work-life balance

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:12.260
or taking the research
in a certain direction

00:48:12.260 --> 00:48:16.360
someone you feel you're going
to be comfortable working with

00:48:16.360 --> 00:48:19.350
that's one of the prime ways

00:48:19.350 --> 00:48:22.860
that you can avoid problems downstream.

00:48:22.860 --> 00:48:26.740
And sometimes that's tough
to tell when you're applying

00:48:26.740 --> 00:48:28.900
I think sometimes you don't
know until you're there

00:48:28.900 --> 00:48:30.490
but it's certainly something,

00:48:30.490 --> 00:48:31.430
this is one of the reasons

00:48:31.430 --> 00:48:34.450
why if there are only one or two people

00:48:34.450 --> 00:48:37.680
at an institution that you
think you want to work with

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:41.960
you should be talking to
them before you even apply

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:46.240
to see whether their interpersonal style

00:48:46.240 --> 00:48:47.823
and yours are gonna mesh.

00:48:50.670 --> 00:48:53.250
- I'd add just one other
quick comment on this topic

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:54.533
maybe before we move on.

00:48:54.533 --> 00:48:56.270
I wanted to actually to emphasize

00:48:56.270 --> 00:48:58.700
some of the things that Ann
and Florian mentioned earlier

00:48:58.700 --> 00:48:59.560
about the importance

00:48:59.560 --> 00:49:01.840
of knowing what resources
are available to you

00:49:01.840 --> 00:49:03.210
and asking for help when you need it

00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:05.982
and so far as a mechanism
to fight off stress.

00:49:05.982 --> 00:49:07.920
So for instance, in my own experience

00:49:07.920 --> 00:49:11.820
and in many other graduate
students' experiences,

00:49:11.820 --> 00:49:14.170
one challenging thing to
deal with in graduate school

00:49:14.170 --> 00:49:16.670
can be imposters syndrome, as one example,

00:49:16.670 --> 00:49:17.820
where you feel like, you know

00:49:17.820 --> 00:49:19.640
maybe your research isn't exciting enough

00:49:19.640 --> 00:49:20.680
or you're not doing well enough

00:49:20.680 --> 00:49:22.420
or you're not smart enough
to be in the program

00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:25.800
this kind of thing is it
can be a common sentiment.

00:49:25.800 --> 00:49:27.040
And I think for me personally

00:49:27.040 --> 00:49:29.580
this is something I struggled
with early on in the program

00:49:29.580 --> 00:49:33.150
'cause I didn't realize all
the resources I had access to

00:49:33.150 --> 00:49:35.300
I wasn't talking to the
older members in my group

00:49:35.300 --> 00:49:37.720
to try and get help when
I was stuck on problems.

00:49:37.720 --> 00:49:40.340
I wasn't, you know, leveraging
some of the other resources

00:49:40.340 --> 00:49:41.770
that were available to me in my group

00:49:41.770 --> 00:49:44.660
and others in the
Quantum Center and so on.

00:49:44.660 --> 00:49:46.020
And so I do think, you know

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:48.380
I just wanted to emphasize
from personal experience

00:49:48.380 --> 00:49:51.570
that knowing about the resources
that are available to you

00:49:51.570 --> 00:49:53.870
and an understanding that
it is the expectation

00:49:53.870 --> 00:49:56.630
that it takes time to learn
about a topic in a field

00:49:56.630 --> 00:49:59.070
then it's okay to not know
things and ask questions

00:49:59.070 --> 00:50:01.540
and you know, this is all important things

00:50:01.540 --> 00:50:03.000
that everyone has to do at some point.

00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:05.159
So for me, it took a while
to come to that realization

00:50:05.159 --> 00:50:08.160
and that was stressful, but you know

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:09.960
having other people around
to help you through that

00:50:09.960 --> 00:50:11.710
it can be a really important thing.

00:50:13.560 --> 00:50:17.120
- Okay, we have a lot more
question about fundings.

00:50:17.120 --> 00:50:19.500
So there's different way of funding.

00:50:19.500 --> 00:50:21.920
So we talked about how
to get funding through TA

00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:24.150
but there's also fellowship and anything.

00:50:24.150 --> 00:50:26.920
Connor, you had fellowship with NSF

00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:29.293
can you talk, tell us a little bit more,

00:50:30.690 --> 00:50:34.140
what was the process and
how you got that one?

00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:34.973
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:50:34.973 --> 00:50:37.660
So most of my funding while
I was a graduate student

00:50:37.660 --> 00:50:40.320
came through an NSF Graduate
Research Fellowship.

00:50:40.320 --> 00:50:42.980
And so the way that worked was during,

00:50:42.980 --> 00:50:45.070
it was actually my second
year of graduate school

00:50:45.070 --> 00:50:46.890
I submitted an application during the fall

00:50:46.890 --> 00:50:48.280
where I proposed the kind of research

00:50:48.280 --> 00:50:49.670
I would be doing for my PhD

00:50:49.670 --> 00:50:51.660
and there were other components as well.

00:50:51.660 --> 00:50:52.580
And they came back

00:50:52.580 --> 00:50:55.740
and offered me funding for
three more years after that.

00:50:55.740 --> 00:50:57.800
So then in the subsequent three years

00:50:57.800 --> 00:50:59.980
that is where my funding came from

00:50:59.980 --> 00:51:02.910
so I didn't have to teach

00:51:02.910 --> 00:51:04.920
and I also didn't have to ask my advisor

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:07.790
to provide me with support
out of one of their grants.

00:51:07.790 --> 00:51:10.150
So the nice aspect of something like that

00:51:10.150 --> 00:51:12.390
is that it can give you a
little bit more flexibility

00:51:12.390 --> 00:51:15.570
to work on whatever it
is you're interested in.

00:51:15.570 --> 00:51:18.690
So when you have your own
funding source, you can, you know

00:51:18.690 --> 00:51:21.400
go to potential advisors and say, you know

00:51:21.400 --> 00:51:23.900
I'm interested in this topic
can you work with me on this?

00:51:23.900 --> 00:51:24.840
And they don't have to worry

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:26.970
about funding money from a
grant to help you with it.

00:51:26.970 --> 00:51:30.210
So it can give you a little bit
more flexibility in that way

00:51:30.210 --> 00:51:32.610
and so I think fellowships in that regard

00:51:32.610 --> 00:51:35.690
can be a useful thing
certainly for you as a student.

00:51:35.690 --> 00:51:37.570
- And you can apply for fellowships

00:51:37.570 --> 00:51:40.930
either as an undergraduate
to use in graduate school

00:51:40.930 --> 00:51:44.680
or while you're in graduate school.

00:51:44.680 --> 00:51:46.800
Usually an undergrad institution

00:51:46.800 --> 00:51:49.280
will have a fellowship's office

00:51:49.280 --> 00:51:51.400
and so you can start with them.

00:51:51.400 --> 00:51:54.970
And then often the graduate school

00:51:54.970 --> 00:51:58.200
will have either an office or a site

00:51:58.200 --> 00:52:01.130
where you can look for fellowships

00:52:01.130 --> 00:52:02.710
that might be pertinent to you.

00:52:02.710 --> 00:52:06.880
There are a number of, sort
of big name fellowships

00:52:06.880 --> 00:52:09.040
that many people get like Fulbright's

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:11.310
which are to travel elsewhere.

00:52:11.310 --> 00:52:13.827
And most campuses will have somebody

00:52:13.827 --> 00:52:15.400
who can help you with that.

00:52:15.400 --> 00:52:18.390
I'd also say that the writing center

00:52:18.390 --> 00:52:20.130
can help you with applications

00:52:20.130 --> 00:52:22.430
even if there isn't a fellowship office.

00:52:22.430 --> 00:52:24.660
So there are a lot of different ways

00:52:24.660 --> 00:52:26.060
that you can find funding.

00:52:26.060 --> 00:52:28.430
But I wanna repeat that if you're applying

00:52:28.430 --> 00:52:31.020
to say an R1 institution, like, Yale

00:52:33.470 --> 00:52:35.983
you're going to get five years of funding.

00:52:37.390 --> 00:52:41.250
You know, it may be that you
have to find a research group

00:52:41.250 --> 00:52:44.220
that will accept you but I would say

00:52:47.500 --> 00:52:50.800
there are very few instances I know of

00:52:50.800 --> 00:52:52.620
where someone had to leave graduate school

00:52:52.620 --> 00:52:54.580
because they had no funding.

00:52:54.580 --> 00:52:57.330
So that's something to
just reassure people on.

00:52:57.330 --> 00:53:00.140
You know, it's something
that you wanna be aware of

00:53:00.140 --> 00:53:05.120
but chances are once you
have gotten into a program

00:53:05.120 --> 00:53:09.920
if it's an R1, a Research 1 institution

00:53:09.920 --> 00:53:13.640
unless you have a major
disagreement with the single person

00:53:13.640 --> 00:53:17.893
who does research in your
area you should be okay.

00:53:19.510 --> 00:53:22.453
- And there's also a lot
more initiative lately.

00:53:22.453 --> 00:53:27.190
The US Government has created
this national quantum centers.

00:53:27.190 --> 00:53:28.825
We have hubs everywhere

00:53:28.825 --> 00:53:32.740
and so these centers
have a lot of fundings

00:53:32.740 --> 00:53:34.850
and including the fundings

00:53:34.850 --> 00:53:36.960
that they receive from the government

00:53:36.960 --> 00:53:40.620
they have fellowships for
graduate students, for post-docs

00:53:40.620 --> 00:53:44.400
and so you may be able to to
go check on these websites

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:46.160
there's also the national labs

00:53:46.160 --> 00:53:49.120
are often associated with
these hubs, these quantum hubs

00:53:49.120 --> 00:53:50.900
and so you may be able
to find different way

00:53:50.900 --> 00:53:52.210
to have resources.

00:53:52.210 --> 00:53:54.330
I think the easiest way is to
get paid by the grad school

00:53:54.330 --> 00:53:55.890
so you don't have to write anything

00:53:55.890 --> 00:53:58.170
so it's sort of like
be the "carefree thing"

00:53:58.170 --> 00:53:59.640
it's not available to anybody

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:01.890
but those are also other options.

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:04.240
And also I know that sometimes

00:54:06.770 --> 00:54:08.350
university can partner with industry

00:54:08.350 --> 00:54:11.400
and the industry will pay
for a chunk of the funding

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:12.830
so that also happens.

00:54:12.830 --> 00:54:14.340
That happened also a lot in Europe

00:54:14.340 --> 00:54:17.510
there's some of these privates,
Government partnership

00:54:17.510 --> 00:54:19.501
so that's also something
that you can consider

00:54:19.501 --> 00:54:23.400
depending on where you
want to go for grad school.

00:54:23.400 --> 00:54:24.520
So we're almost out of time.

00:54:24.520 --> 00:54:26.080
I think maybe what we can do

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:27.657
is we can take one last question

00:54:27.657 --> 00:54:30.840
talking about the timeline
of applying to grad school.

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:32.370
Like, what are the deadlines

00:54:32.370 --> 00:54:35.520
and what are the time that we
need to take to go through?

00:54:35.520 --> 00:54:38.040
- So I was just typing an
answer to that question

00:54:38.040 --> 00:54:40.815
because it seemed like
one, what I would say

00:54:40.815 --> 00:54:44.600
is our application at Yale opens in August

00:54:44.600 --> 00:54:47.083
and depending upon the program,

00:54:48.810 --> 00:54:52.260
the deadline can be
December 1st, December 15th

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:54.090
or January 1st.

00:54:54.090 --> 00:54:56.490
And so if you are thinking of applying

00:54:56.490 --> 00:54:59.303
you really wanna have
your materials ready.

00:55:01.479 --> 00:55:05.450
I'm gonna try and fold in another question

00:55:05.450 --> 00:55:07.890
which is what do you need to do to apply?

00:55:07.890 --> 00:55:09.500
But let me talk about the timeline

00:55:09.500 --> 00:55:11.900
and then I'll try and tell
you the biggest pieces

00:55:11.900 --> 00:55:13.833
of getting ready to apply.

00:55:15.010 --> 00:55:17.800
So then usually depending upon the program

00:55:17.800 --> 00:55:20.870
there may be an on-campus
visit that is arranged

00:55:20.870 --> 00:55:22.140
or a virtual visit,

00:55:22.140 --> 00:55:25.150
and sometimes some schools do that

00:55:25.150 --> 00:55:27.780
before acceptance decisions are made

00:55:27.780 --> 00:55:30.500
and some schools do it
after decisions are made

00:55:30.500 --> 00:55:33.330
and even that can vary within programs.

00:55:33.330 --> 00:55:36.333
Usually in early spring,

00:55:37.480 --> 00:55:41.230
January and February decisions are made

00:55:41.230 --> 00:55:44.610
and then the students
who have been admitted

00:55:44.610 --> 00:55:47.410
have to make a decision by April 15th.

00:55:47.410 --> 00:55:50.410
So that's the rough
timeline, but I would say go

00:55:50.410 --> 00:55:52.610
if you're thinking of specific schools

00:55:52.610 --> 00:55:54.760
you should go and look
at the school's website

00:55:54.760 --> 00:55:58.250
and they will have a
more specific timeline.

00:55:58.250 --> 00:56:01.100
The things that you wanna
focus on in your application

00:56:01.100 --> 00:56:03.445
are really research,

00:56:03.445 --> 00:56:06.740
in the sciences that's the key issue.

00:56:06.740 --> 00:56:08.760
And it's also the determining issue

00:56:08.760 --> 00:56:11.820
for those who really
thrive in graduate school

00:56:11.820 --> 00:56:12.933
in the sciences.

00:56:13.930 --> 00:56:15.510
Faculty are looking for students

00:56:15.510 --> 00:56:17.763
with prior research experience.

00:56:19.570 --> 00:56:22.940
And you want in your statement of purpose

00:56:22.940 --> 00:56:27.200
which is different from an
undergraduate personal statement,

00:56:27.200 --> 00:56:28.750
to really answer questions

00:56:28.750 --> 00:56:33.060
about why do you wanna
come to graduate school?

00:56:33.060 --> 00:56:35.190
What are the questions or problems

00:56:35.190 --> 00:56:36.610
that really interests you?

00:56:36.610 --> 00:56:40.970
What have you done to
prepare to work on these?

00:56:40.970 --> 00:56:45.100
And why is the institution
that you're planning to come to

00:56:45.100 --> 00:56:48.160
or applying to the right
place for you to do this?

00:56:48.160 --> 00:56:51.261
And that means knowing who the faculty are

00:56:51.261 --> 00:56:54.770
and what kind of research
they're working on.

00:56:54.770 --> 00:56:57.240
So the statement of purpose is really key

00:56:57.240 --> 00:57:00.380
and prior research experience
is also really key.

00:57:00.380 --> 00:57:02.810
I would say the third piece
that's really important

00:57:02.810 --> 00:57:04.473
is letters of recommendation,

00:57:05.360 --> 00:57:06.730
because those are the,

00:57:06.730 --> 00:57:10.990
and you wanna get them as at
least three strong letters

00:57:12.080 --> 00:57:14.850
from people who know your work,

00:57:14.850 --> 00:57:16.590
have seen you do research

00:57:16.590 --> 00:57:18.490
and know you as a person.

00:57:18.490 --> 00:57:19.880
And I could say more about that

00:57:19.880 --> 00:57:22.693
but I noticed the time and
I don't wanna keep anybody.

00:57:23.940 --> 00:57:25.480
- Sounds good, but almost out of time,

00:57:25.480 --> 00:57:30.480
maybe while I flash the
next events for the series

00:57:30.890 --> 00:57:33.180
maybe we can go round
and have a last few words

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:36.030
from each of you and the last advice

00:57:36.030 --> 00:57:39.290
that you can give people
considering grad school.

00:57:39.290 --> 00:57:41.900
- Advice broadly for people
considering grad school

00:57:41.900 --> 00:57:43.590
is a well, a very broad category.

00:57:43.590 --> 00:57:44.898
So I don't know that I can say something

00:57:44.898 --> 00:57:47.540
that's gonna be of
interest to everyone here.

00:57:47.540 --> 00:57:51.780
But I think one thing that
for me was very helpful

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:54.800
in this process was just
speaking to students

00:57:54.800 --> 00:57:56.950
at the different programs
I was looking at.

00:57:56.950 --> 00:57:58.390
You know, a lot of the questions

00:57:58.390 --> 00:57:59.530
I think have been posed here

00:57:59.530 --> 00:58:02.740
are things that people who
have been through the program

00:58:02.740 --> 00:58:03.820
can readily answer for you

00:58:03.820 --> 00:58:06.050
and really speak to how it
affected their experience

00:58:06.050 --> 00:58:08.840
whether these different factors
were positive or negatives.

00:58:08.840 --> 00:58:11.180
And so, you know, I
think talking to students

00:58:11.180 --> 00:58:13.100
as you look at different programs

00:58:13.100 --> 00:58:15.210
should be a crucial determining factor

00:58:15.210 --> 00:58:17.450
in gathering information.

00:58:17.450 --> 00:58:18.930
So not only can you talk to people

00:58:18.930 --> 00:58:21.170
about the structure of
the graduate program

00:58:21.170 --> 00:58:22.560
or the department as a whole,

00:58:22.560 --> 00:58:23.885
but you can also talk to people

00:58:23.885 --> 00:58:25.710
in a particular research group

00:58:25.710 --> 00:58:26.930
to get a sense of what it's like

00:58:26.930 --> 00:58:29.110
to work with a particular advisor,

00:58:29.110 --> 00:58:30.530
is the environment stressful,

00:58:30.530 --> 00:58:32.630
what are the expectations on the students?

00:58:33.690 --> 00:58:36.080
You know, I think this is all
information you can gather

00:58:36.080 --> 00:58:39.170
before you accept or even
apply to certain programs

00:58:39.170 --> 00:58:40.650
to try and whittle down your choices.

00:58:40.650 --> 00:58:41.910
And so for me, at least

00:58:41.910 --> 00:58:43.930
certainly this was really
valuable information.

00:58:43.930 --> 00:58:45.990
And for those considering graduate school

00:58:45.990 --> 00:58:48.670
I would say if you're on the
fence about different programs

00:58:48.670 --> 00:58:50.000
or different areas of research

00:58:50.000 --> 00:58:52.120
definitely talk to some
of the students there

00:58:52.120 --> 00:58:54.683
they can certainly help point
you in the right direction.

00:58:58.830 --> 00:59:00.230
- Yeah, I think that just,

00:59:00.230 --> 00:59:03.190
even if you're embarrassed to
ask a question about something

00:59:03.190 --> 00:59:04.490
like it's better to ask

00:59:04.490 --> 00:59:08.490
and have someone hopefully
answer it for you.

00:59:08.490 --> 00:59:11.480
So emailing professors that
you wanna work with them,

00:59:11.480 --> 00:59:15.430
emailing like other people

00:59:15.430 --> 00:59:16.977
who have positions in the
department, stuff like that,

00:59:16.977 --> 00:59:19.150
that's a great way to gather information.

00:59:19.150 --> 00:59:23.680
And even just like
directly emailing students

00:59:23.680 --> 00:59:25.140
is a great way to get information.

00:59:25.140 --> 00:59:28.070
Or if you can visit onsite,

00:59:28.070 --> 00:59:29.880
like that's like walking around

00:59:29.880 --> 00:59:33.493
and talking to people in
person, it's totally worth it.

00:59:35.430 --> 00:59:36.860
But if you're emailing professors

00:59:36.860 --> 00:59:38.800
and they aren't replying
do not take it personally

00:59:38.800 --> 00:59:42.320
I know that lots of them just
have so many emails every day

00:59:42.320 --> 00:59:43.410
and like I have a hard time

00:59:43.410 --> 00:59:45.470
getting responses from my advisor

00:59:45.470 --> 00:59:47.500
sometimes depending on how busy he is.

00:59:47.500 --> 00:59:49.940
So if you're really interested

00:59:49.940 --> 00:59:51.624
with working with someone in particular,

00:59:51.624 --> 00:59:54.420
I would send follow-up
emails and things like that

00:59:54.420 --> 00:59:56.350
without thinking you're bugging them

00:59:56.350 --> 00:59:58.270
or thinking that they're ignoring you,

00:59:58.270 --> 01:00:00.120
it's much more likely that it's just lost

01:00:00.120 --> 01:00:02.233
in the stream of emails they get.

01:00:03.860 --> 01:00:08.093
- So I would say don't be
afraid to aim high, you know

01:00:09.860 --> 01:00:11.763
ask a lot of questions,

01:00:12.670 --> 01:00:15.110
do your homework and your research

01:00:15.110 --> 01:00:17.760
to find out who's doing what where

01:00:17.760 --> 01:00:22.130
and don't be afraid to ask for help

01:00:22.130 --> 01:00:24.013
at any stage along the way.

01:00:25.800 --> 01:00:27.450
- I'll just echo what
everybody else has said.

01:00:27.450 --> 01:00:29.180
It's really important to do your homework

01:00:29.180 --> 01:00:33.420
in terms of, you know, or do
as much homework as you can

01:00:33.420 --> 01:00:35.550
because once you make the choice, you know

01:00:35.550 --> 01:00:37.990
you're kinda there and you
wanna make the best of it

01:00:37.990 --> 01:00:41.070
and not have to switch
or anything like that.

01:00:41.070 --> 01:00:42.860
So doing all your homework ahead of time

01:00:42.860 --> 01:00:44.680
will really help with all that

01:00:44.680 --> 01:00:46.730
and asking all those important questions.

01:00:48.340 --> 01:00:49.240
- Perfect.

01:00:49.240 --> 01:00:50.930
Thank you very much all of you,

01:00:50.930 --> 01:00:52.310
thank you for all the attendees

01:00:52.310 --> 01:00:54.480
who came in and asked the question.

01:00:54.480 --> 01:00:56.120
This is will be recorded

01:00:56.120 --> 01:00:59.482
and put later on on our websites.

01:00:59.482 --> 01:01:02.210
So you'll be able to have
all the answers again

01:01:02.210 --> 01:01:03.520
if you missed something.

01:01:03.520 --> 01:01:06.170
Thank you all to our panelists
and have a good night.

